{
  "beruf": "hotel",
  "title": "Hotels and tourism",
  "level": "A2–B1",
  "reviewed": false,
  "_note": "italian-for-work run, 2026-11-15: written by hand for Italy, not yet read by a human or a second reader. translations.en = English meaning; deHint = German translation. Working conditions are general orientation only; facts marked 'to verify' in the run report.",
  "intro": "Tourism is one of Italy's biggest employers, from hotels and B&Bs to agriturismi in the countryside, and much of the work is seasonal. This deck has reception and rooms, the work of a cameriera ai piani, bookings and check-in, safety and cleaning, and what to say to guests and your team. Use Lei with guests unless they offer tu.",
  "introEasy": "Do you work in a hotel or in tourism in Italy? Learn the words for your work here.",
  "jobs": [
    "Cameriera/e ai piani",
    "Addetto/a alla reception",
    "Portiere di notte",
    "Facchino",
    "Addetto/a alle pulizie in strutture ricettive",
    "Animatore/animatrice turistico/a"
  ],
  "tipps": [
    "At reception a smile and four sentences go a long way: «Buongiorno, benvenuti!», «A che nome è la prenotazione?», «Mi lascia un documento, per favore?», «La sua camera è la 214, al secondo piano.»",
    "Before you enter a room say «Permesso? Servizio in camera.» and wait for an answer. If the card «Non disturbare» hangs on the door, do not knock.",
    "Hotels in Italy have to register every guest from an ID document and report the data to the police, so you must ask for a passport or a carta d'identità at check-in. Explain it politely, copy only what is needed and never leave documents lying around.",
    "The tassa di soggiorno is a small tax per person and night that each comune sets for itself, so the amount differs from town to town. Guests often ask why they pay it: «È una tassa del comune, non dell'albergo.»",
    "Many jobs in tourism are lavoro stagionale, with a contratto a tempo determinato for the season. Pay, hours, ferie, straordinario and the tredicesima depend on your contract and the national agreement (CCNL Turismo). Ask the employer, a union (sindacato/patronato) or the Ispettorato del lavoro. This is general orientation, not legal advice."
  ],
